180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E (Planarity detection is extremely useful to set base guidelines for the beginning of a PCB design by returning a theoretical minimum number of layers. Our implementation returns the minimum number of layers that would be required for a given electrical layout on a PCB by determining the planarity of the layout and continually making subgraphs of non-planar components. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E (We ultimately used a Hamiltonian Cycle planarity detection algorithm, however we evaluated other programs such as DMP and K3,3/K5 algorithms. We discuss why we chose this algorithm and drawbacks/benefits of all the programs. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 165 0 R
/E (Electrical components on a PCB can be represented as a graph, where verticies are components and edges are traces \(connections\) between components. The number of layers that a certain schematic requires can be determined by computing the planarity of the schematic, where a nonplanar schematic means more than one layer is required. To determine a theoretical minimum number of layers required, an algorithm could iteratively take nonplanar subgraphs of a larger schematic, running planarity tests until a subgraph is planar. The total number of subgraph divisions required is then the minimum number of layers. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
