255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 168 0 R
/E (We do not have limitations with what can be represented, as all connections in a PCB can be abstracted as vertices and edges. This algorithm that we have implemented will tell you the minimum number of layers necessary, but that may not be best practice for professional PCB design)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 698 0 R
/E (In our code, when a graph returns as nonplanar it also returns the positions of the original vertices \(representing where component pins are on the PCB\) and the leftover edges that make it nonplanar.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 699 0 R
/E (It takes the the leftover edges and the positions of the vertices and recursively passes it back through the process of finding a hamiltonian cycle, labeling edges Inside \(I\), Outside \(O\) or Leftover, and then repeats this recursion until it has all planar subgraphs. Each time it repeats the recursion it adds +1 to layer count.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
